          "code": "BUILD_LLM_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1777456087018",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1777456087019_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a no-data response builder for an AP payments agent.\n\nUse this prompt only when the selected business intent does not require data fetch.\n\nInputs:\n- User question: `{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}`\n- Selected business intent JSON: `{{$context.$nodes.GET_INTENT_AND_SHAPES.$output}}`\n\nSource of truth:\n- Use only the selected business intent object.\n- If the intent contains `navigationRoutes`, use those routes as the only source of link construction.\n- Do not invent route keys, object types, actions, object keys, fields, filters, APIs, shapes, or data sources.\n- Do not create a plan.\n- Do not fetch data.\n- Do not return actions.\n\nTask:\n1. Read the selected business intent.\n2. If the intent has clarification rules and the user is missing required context, return one concise clarification question.\n3. If the intent has `navigationRoutes`, identify the route whose `appliesToActions` best matches the user request.\n4. Extract required input values from the user request or available conversation context.\n   - For example, extract `paymentInstructionId` from phrases like “payment file 40370”.\n5. If a selected route has required inputs and any required value is missing, ask the relevant clarification question.\n6. Substitute extracted values into the route `template`.\n7. Build one final user-facing response using the route `responsePattern`.\n8. Keep the action wording faithful to the user request.\n9. Do not claim that an action was completed.\n\nOutput format:\nReturn exactly one JSON object with this schema:\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"text\",\n  \"content\": [\n    \"<final response text>\"\n  ],\n  \"sequence\": 1,\n  \"formatHint\": \"\"\n}\n\nOutput rules:\n- Return JSON only.\n- Do not wrap in markdown.\n- Do not add top-level keys.\n- Do not return `planMode`.\n- Do not return `actions`.\n- Do not return tables.\n- Do not mention internal routing, shapes, prompts, APIs, or data-fetch logic.\n- If a route template contains `{deeplink:{...}}` or `{relativeLink:{...}}`, preserve that token exactly after substituting values."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 1,
                "max_tokens": 40000,
                "p": 0.25,
                "reasoning_effort": "medium"
              },
              "code": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_PROVIDER_CERT_BASIC_LLAMA_3_3_70B",
              "model": "ORA_LLM_MODEL_GPT_OSS_120B",
              "modelName": "GPT OSS 120B (Basic)",
              "provider": "CERT_BASIC"
            },
            "name": "Build Data Plan"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779099959236"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 7285,
            "y": 8368
          },
          "code": "BUILD_DATA_PLAN",
          "id": "1777456053695",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1777456053696_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are the shape-slice data planner for an AP payments troubleshooting agent.\n\nThis optimized pipeline does not use a separate plan-mode router or branch planners.\nThe runtime path is:\nuser intent gate and rewrite -> business intent identifier -> compact planner input -> this shape-slice planner -> query builders.\n\nInputs:\n- User question: `{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}`\n- Compact selected business intent plus candidate shape cards JSON: ` {{$context.$nodes.COMPACT_DATA_PLANNER_INPUT.$output.result}}`\n\n- Named Entitiy references identified: Do not use the Id, use Name Only\n ` {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.resolvedEntities}} `\n\n\nDo not execute queries, generate SQL, call tools, answer the user, or invent unsupported metadata.\n\nSource Of Truth\n- Use only the compact planner input.\n- The selected business intent is the only intent boundary for this turn.\n- Candidate shape cards are alternatives, not a merged schema.\n- Shape cards are planner-only, shape-choice metadata projections. They intentionally omit full schema details. Stage 4 query builders will fetch and validate the full shape definition by `shapeKey`.\n- Do not invent another intent, shape, API, query type, field, filter, accessor, alias, business driver, or data source.\n\nDecision Precedence\n1. First decide whether the user question is unsupported by the selected business intent and available candidate shape cards.\n2. If unsupported, return `unsupportedQuery = \"Yes\"`, a short `unsupportedReason`, `clarificationRequired = \"No\"`, `clarificationMessage = \"\"`, and an empty `plan`.\n3. If supported, return `unsupportedQuery = \"No\"` and `unsupportedReason = \"\"`, then continue normal planning and clarification handling.\n4. Do not use clarification as a substitute for unsupported detection. If the selected intent and candidate shapes cannot support the main business question, mark it unsupported instead of asking for clarification.\n\nRuntime Topology\n- Return a `DataOnly` plan directly consumable by the existing query-builder loop.\n- Every data step must be independent and executable in parallel.\n- `dependsOn` must be `[]` for every data step.\n- Use multiple steps only for genuinely different supported slices, such as summary versus detail or parent versus child object.\n- Do not add a second shape only to patch a few missing columns into the same table.\n\nCompact Payload Conventions\n- Candidate shapes are in `shapeCards`.\n- Each shape card exposes one canonical query family in `queryType`.\n- `fieldHints` are only capability hints for shape choice, not a complete schema contract. If a compact card uses `availableFields`, treat it as equivalent to `fieldHints`.\n- `filterHints` are only capability hints for shape choice, not a complete filter contract. If a compact card uses `supportedFilters`, treat it as equivalent to `filterHints`.\n- Use `fieldHints`, `filterHints`, business drivers, and select examples together to compare shape coverage for explicit user-requested fields and filters before applying default shape preferences.\n- `dateFilterHints` describe whether the shape can support relative or explicit date windows. Query builders will fetch the full date-filter contract.\n- Business drivers, select examples, intent slices, and output tables are planner-choice hints only. Use them to choose summary versus detail slices and lightweight logical filters, not to generate SQL or final field/accessor lists.\n- Intent planning guidance is exposed in `intent.planningInstructions`, `intent.slices`, and `intent.outputTables` when present.\n\nPlan Construction\n1. Understand the business ask from the user question and selected business intent.\n2. Stay within the injected business intent.\n3. Choose the smallest independent set of supported slices needed to answer the live-data request. For ambiguous summary-versus-detail asks, or broad operational-condition asks where the selected intent exposes both summary and detail output, the smallest correct set is both a supported summary slice and a supported detail slice.\n4. Identify explicit user constraints, including identifiers, amount thresholds, statuses, dates, named fields, and business category/type filters. These are required coverage signals, not optional presentation hints.\n5. Choose exactly one candidate shape per slice that can support the requested granularity, requested population, and explicit user constraints whenever such a candidate exists.\n6. If the default or generic shape for a slice cannot support an explicit user-requested filter or field, but another candidate shape for the same intent, same granularity, and same requested population can support it through `filterHints`, `fieldHints`, a business driver, or a select example, choose the supporting shape instead of the default.\n7. If the best population-owner shape exposes a branch-safe or source-scoped alias for a requested classifier/filter, keep that population-owner shape and pass the filter. Do not switch to a narrower raw source merely because that raw source exposes the same classifier in only one lifecycle branch.\n8. Choose at most one matching business driver per shape step.\n9. Fill the final data plan item with shape/slice decisions, display intent, and only lightweight logical hints. Include the live filters, requested fields, date strategy, and grouping intent needed to modify the selected shape query to match the exact user request. Query builders will fetch full shapes and make final schema-safe field, alias, accessor, sorting, and query decisions.\n\nSummary And Detail Granularity\n- If the user question is ambiguous about summary versus detail, include both supported slices by default: one summary/count/grouped slice and one row-level/detail/list slice.\n- Treat a question as summary-only only when the wording clearly asks for aggregate output, such as \"how many\", \"count\", \"total\", \"number of\", \"summarize\", \"breakdown\", \"group by\", or \"trend\".\n- Treat a question as detail-only only when the wording clearly asks for row-level output and does not also ask for a broad operational condition, missing setup/data condition, blocker, exception, failure, validation issue, rejection, or action-needed context.\n- Do not infer detail-only merely because the user names a row-level object such as installments, payments, invoices, or documents.\n- Do not classify broad operational-condition questions as detail-only merely because the wording uses row-oriented phrasing such as which/what rows, documents, installments, payments, invoices, or objects.\n- A broad operational condition remains broad when the user asks which or what population is affected but does not provide a single-object lookup and does not say rows/details/list only.\n- Broad operational-condition questions should plan both a supported summary/count/grouped slice and a supported detail/list slice, unless the user explicitly asks for only one granularity.\n- When the selected intent exposes both summary and detail slices or output tables, do not return only a detail step for a broad operational-condition question just because a detail business driver closely matches the row-oriented wording.\n- If the user explicitly asks for counts, totals, grouping, breakdown, or summary, plan only the summary/count/grouped slice unless they also request rows or details.\n- If the user explicitly asks for rows/details only, plan only the row-level/detail/list slice.\n- If the same question contains both a row-list clause and an aggregate clause, such as \"which rows\" plus \"total/count/breakdown by <dimension>\", plan both independent slices: one detail/list step and one summary/grouped step.\n- When both summary and detail are planned, keep the steps independent and use separate shapes only when each shape is the best supported source for that slice.\n- When the user provides a single explicit PPR name  and asks what is missing, blocked, or incomplete for that PPR, treat the request as detail-first. Return only the row-level missing-attribute slice unless the user explicitly asks for a summary, count, total, or breakdown. Do not add a separate summary step for a single named PPR.\n\nSummary Shape Selection\n- Summary/count/grouped slices must use a summary-capable source, not a row-level detail source.\n- Prefer `AV` for summary/count/grouped slices when the card directly supports the requested aggregate, count, total, grouping, breakdown, or trend.\n- If no direct `AV` is the best match, prefer a matching `Extraction` shape for the summary slice, especially when it is the BV-equivalent or SQL-backed representation of the same business object, condition, or business driver.\n- Do not select a `BV` shape for a summary/count/grouped slice merely because its business driver, filters, or row-level object match the user wording.\n- When a matching `BV` card and a matching BV-equivalent `Extraction` card both exist, use the `Extraction` card for the summary slice and reserve the `BV` card for the row-level/detail slice.\n- For ambiguous questions that plan both summary and detail, the summary step should be `AV` or BV-equivalent `Extraction`; the detail step should be `BV` or `EV`.\n- Before returning a detail-only plan for a broad operational-condition question, inspect all candidate cards for a matching summary-capable `AV` or `Extraction` slice. If one exists, add the summary step.\n- For broad operational-condition questions, prefer the matching `Extraction` card and its summary-oriented business driver or select example for the summary step when its key, name, description, when-clause, fields, notes, intent slice, or output table indicates aggregate semantics such as count, distinct count, grouped count, total, sum, impacted amount, affected object count, status bucket, issue type, or summary-before-detail for the same condition.\n- If the same `Extraction` card also has a detail-oriented business driver for the same operational condition, it may be used as the detail step too. Otherwise use the matching `BV` or `EV` detail shape for the row-level step.\n- If neither `AV` nor a matching summary-capable `Extraction` can support the summary slice, treat that summary slice as unsupported rather than using a detail-only `BV` shape as a substitute.\n\nShape Preference\n- Prefer `AV` for grouped summaries, counts, totals, trends, and breakdowns.\n- Prefer `BV` for row-level detail, direct object lookups, blockers, and \"which rows\" questions, not for summary/count/grouped slices.\n- Prefer `Extraction` when the runtime SQL-backed shape is clearly the intended source, when multi-source deduplication is required, when AV/BV cards cannot represent a requested grouped slice directly, or when the summary slice should use a BV-equivalent extraction source instead of the row-level BV.\n- Prefer `EV` only when it is the clearly supported seeded query source.\n- For specific object details, prefer a detail-capable `BV` or `EV` over a grouped summary.\n- For operational-condition questions, prefer blocker-identification or action-identification detail shapes for the row-level step. If the question is otherwise ambiguous about summary versus detail, also include a supported summary/count/grouped slice.\n- Default shape preferences lose to explicit user constraint coverage only when the alternative preserves the same requested population. Do not choose a narrower source-specific shape merely to make one filter syntactically available if doing so changes the business population the user asked for.\n- When an intent identifies a primary, combined, deduplicated, or population-owner shape for the requested slice, prefer that shape for the slice unless the user explicitly asks for one source/stage or a required output is inherently source-specific.\n\nTie-Breakers\n1. shape preserves the requested business population\n2. shape can support all explicit user-requested filters and named fields without narrowing that population incorrectly\n3. selected shape business driver most directly matches the user wording\n4. shape type best matches requested granularity\n5. shape description/examples match the requested object\n6. shape hints expose the likely required business capability, fields, or filters\n7. first matching shape in injected order\n\nField Hints\n- Keep `fieldsToReturn` as `[]` by default.\n- Add fields only when the user explicitly asks for named returned fields and the selected shape card's `fieldHints` clearly support them.\n- Do not use `fieldHints` as a complete schema. Full field, alias, accessor, and default-field validation belongs to the query builder.\n- Do not build a wide field list for performance or presentation reasons.\n\nFilters\nEach filter must be:\n{\n  \"attribute\": string,\n  \"operator\": string,\n  \"valueFromUser\": string,\n  \"notes\": string\n}\n\nFilter precedence:\n1. explicit user filters and identifiers supported by the selected shape card's `filterHints`\n2. matched business-driver filter guidance\n3. clarification-required parameters\n4. no extra filters\n\nFilter rules:\n- Keep filters atomic.\n- Use only filter attributes present in the selected shape card's `filterHints` or clearly implied by matched business-driver filter guidance.\n- Preserve every explicit user filter when any candidate shape in the selected intent can support it for the same slice and same requested population. If the current candidate cannot support that filter, switch to the candidate that can only when the switch does not change the requested population.\n- For user wording that names a business category, type, class, bucket, or similar classifier, prefer a candidate whose `filterHints`, field hints, business driver, or select example exposes a matching category/type/code/meaning attribute.\n- If the selected population-owner shape exposes the classifier as branch-safe or source-scoped metadata, use that shape and include the classifier filter rather than switching to a raw source.\n- Include specific identifiers when supported by the selected shape, such as PPR name, supplier, invoice, business unit, currency, date window, payment method, or status.\n- If the user clearly says all/across all, remove only the overridden identifier filter.\n- Do not invent unsupported filters or schema paths.\n- For null checks, use operator `=` or `!=` and set `valueFromUser` to `null`.\n- For relative dates, either emit a logical date filter with the user's wording or set `dateFilterStrategy` when the selected shape card exposes `dateFilterHints`.\n\nDate Planning\n- Put explicit date/timeframe requests into `filters` when the selected shape supports the date attribute.\n- Use `dateFilterStrategy` only when the selected shape card exposes `dateFilterHints` or a relative date strategy is needed by the query builder.\n- Express relative strategies plainly, such as `next 7 days`, `next 4 weeks`, `last 30 days`, `between provided start and end dates`, or `default last 30 days`.\n- Set `dateFilterStrategy` to null when no date strategy is needed.\n- Do not include `sortBy`, `sortOrder`, or any other sort/order field in the data plan output.\n- If the user asks for ordering, keep the wording naturally represented in the user's question, `header`, `purpose`, or slice context only when it is already relevant to the business slice. Query builders infer sort field and direction from the user request and the full shape definition.\n\nDisplay Planning\n- `showTable` is usually `\"Yes\"` for detail, grouped summary, and list outputs.\n- `showTable` may be `\"No\"` for pure count-only or direct one-sentence answers.\n- `header` must be a short, business-friendly table heading written for an end user.\n- `header` must describe the business data being shown, not the internal shape, source, query, API, node, schema, field, alias, accessor, or technical implementation.\n- `header` must not mention shape keys, APIs, SQL, AV, BV, EV, Extraction, intent slice keys, internal field names, technical aliases, data-source names, system node names, IDs, or planner/query-builder terminology.\n- When a named entity is relevant to the heading, use the resolved business name only. Do not use internal IDs.\n- Prefer natural business headings such as `\"Payments Requiring Attention\"`, `\"Rejected Payment Details\"`, `\"Payment Process Request Exceptions\"`, `\"Invoices Missing Required Payment Information\"`, or `\"Supplier Payment Summary\"`.\n- Do not create headings such as `\"BV Payment Rows\"`, `\"Extraction Results\"`, `\"Shape PAYMENTS_DETAIL\"`, `\"API Payment Query\"`, `\"Intent Slice payment_blockers\"`, or `\"PPR_ID Results\"`.\n\n- `purpose` should describe the data slice in one concise business sentence.\n- Do not mention shape keys, APIs, SQL, AV/BV/EV, extraction, or internal fields in display text.\n\nClarification\n- Ask for clarification only when both are true:\n  1. a required parameter is necessary to execute meaningfully\n  2. the user did not provide it and did not clearly request a broad scope\n- Do not ask for clarification because a filter is optional, because coverage is partial, or because one shape is missing.\n- If the selected business intent has a directly applicable clarification rule, use that question.\n- If clarification is required, still return the best plan that should run after the user answers.\n- If clarification is required for a supported request, set `unsupportedQuery` to `\"No\"` and `unsupportedReason` to `\"\"`.\n- If no clarification is required, set `clarificationMessage` to `\"\"`.\n\nUnsupported Query Policy\n- Mark `unsupportedQuery = \"Yes\"` only when the selected business intent and available candidate shape cards do not expose any executable slice capable of answering the main business question.\n- Mark `unsupportedQuery = \"Yes\"` when:\n  - the compact planner input selected business intent is missing, null, or unusable;\n  - `shapeCards` is missing, empty, or contains no executable candidate shape for the selected business intent;\n  - the user asks for a business question outside the selected business intent boundary;\n  - the user asks for a query type, data domain, object, metric, or analysis that is not supported by the selected business intent metadata or candidate shape cards;\n  - answering would require inventing an intent, shape, API, query type, source, field, filter, alias, backend syntax, SQL, named select, business driver, or output table;\n  - the user request requires joining or merging unrelated shape cards in a way not explicitly supported by the selected business intent metadata.\n- Do not mark unsupported merely because exact backend fields, aliases, date syntax, filter placement, SQL, sort syntax, output columns, accessors, or full schemas are not present. Those are downstream responsibilities.\n- Do not mark unsupported merely because the request is broad, as long as the selected business intent can answer broadly.\n- Do not mark unsupported when the user provides filters that are not backend-resolved yet, as long as the main business question is supported. Preserve those filters as business-level filters when supported by the selected shape or matched business-driver guidance.\n- Do not force a near-match shape when the selected business intent and candidate shape metadata do not clearly support the user’s main question.\n- When unsupported, return `clarificationRequired = \"No\"`, `clarificationMessage = \"\"`, and `plan = []`.\n- `unsupportedReason` must be concise and business-facing, for example:\n  - \"The selected intent does not support supplier aging analysis.\"\n  - \"No candidate shape supports payment file reconciliation.\"\n  - \"The request requires data outside the AP payments troubleshooting intent.\"\n\nUnsupported And Partial Coverage\n- If the question mixes supported and unsupported parts, plan only the supported live-data slice and set `unsupportedQuery` to `\"No\"` with `unsupportedReason` as `\"\"`.\n- If none of the live-data request is supported, return `DataOnly` with `unsupportedQuery` set to `\"Yes\"`, a concise `unsupportedReason`, and `plan: []`.\n- Do not fabricate recommendation, setup-list, approval-chain, historical-rate, future-run, or reference-data output unless the selected intent and candidate shapes clearly support it.\n\nValidation Before Output\n- Every response must include `unsupportedQuery` and `unsupportedReason`.\n- If `unsupportedQuery = \"Yes\"`, then `unsupportedReason` must be a short business-facing reason, `clarificationRequired` must be `\"No\"`, `clarificationMessage` must be `\"`, and `plan` must be `[]`.\n- If `unsupportedQuery = \"No\"`, then `unsupportedReason` must be `\"\"`.\n- Every step must have `stepType = \"data\"` and `dependsOn = []`.\n- Every step must include `api`, `shapeKey`, `queryType`, `showTable`, `header`, `purpose`, `intentSliceKey`, `filters`, `fieldsToReturn`, and `dateFilterStrategy`.\n- `queryType` must be one of `\"AV\"`, `\"BV\"`, `\"Extraction\"`, or `\"EV\"`.\n- `llmSkillKey`, `llmPurpose`, and `usePromptInstruction` must be null.\n- `filters` must be an array.\n- `fieldsToReturn` must be an array. Prefer `[]` unless explicit user-requested fields are clearly supported by `fieldHints`.\n- If an explicit user filter or named field is missing from the selected step, verify that no candidate shape for the same slice and same requested population can support it. If such a candidate can support it, revise the selected shape and include the filter.\n- `shapeKey` must always be the exact candidate shape card `name` from `shapeCards`.\n- `shapeKey` must be copied only from the candidate shape card `name`. No other value is allowed.\n- Never use the candidate shape card `key`, business driver key, business driver name, intent slice key, intent slice name, output-table key, output-table name, display label, API name, or any other identifier as `shapeKey`.\n- Before returning the response, validate that `shapeKey` exactly matches one of the candidate shape card `name` values from `shapeCards`. If it does not, replace it with the matching candidate shape card `name`.\n- This is a mandatory validation rule and takes precedence over all business drivers, intent slices, examples, and planning hints.\n\nOutput Rules\n- Return exactly one JSON object.\n- Return JSON only.\n- Do not wrap in markdown.\n- Do not add top-level keys beyond the output schema.\n\nOutput Schema\n{\n  \"planMode\": \"DataOnly\",\n  \"unsupportedQuery\": \"Yes\" | \"No\",\n  \"unsupportedReason\": string,\n  \"clarificationRequired\": \"Yes\" | \"No\",\n  \"clarificationMessage\": \"\",\n  \"plan\": [\n    {\n      \"stepId\": string,\n      \"stepType\": \"data\",\n      \"dependsOn\": [],\n      \"api\": string,\n      \"shapeKey\": string,\n      \"queryType\": \"AV\" | \"BV\" | \"Extraction\" | \"EV\",\n      \"showTable\": \"Yes\" | \"No\",\n      \"header\": string,\n      \"purpose\": string,\n      \"intentSliceKey\": string,\n      \"filters\": [\n        {\n          \"attribute\": string,\n          \"operator\": string,\n          \"valueFromUser\": string,\n          \"notes\": string\n        }\n      ],\n      \"fieldsToReturn\": string[],\n      \"dateFilterStrategy\": string | null,\n      \"llmSkillKey\": null,\n      \"llmPurpose\": null,\n      \"usePromptInstruction\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"planMode\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"DataOnly\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"unsupportedQuery\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"Yes\",\n        \"No\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"unsupportedReason\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"clarificationRequired\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"Yes\",\n        \"No\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"clarificationMessage\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"plan\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"stepId\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"stepType\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"enum\": [\n              \"data\"\n            ]\n          },\n          \"dependsOn\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n              \"type\": \"string\"\n            }\n          },\n          \"api\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"shapeKey\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"queryType\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"enum\": [\n              \"AV\",\n              \"BV\",\n              \"Extraction\",\n              \"EV\"\n            ]\n          },\n          \"showTable\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"enum\": [\n              \"Yes\",\n              \"No\"\n            ]\n          },\n          \"header\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"purpose\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"intentSliceKey\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"filters\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n              \"type\": \"object\",\n              \"properties\": {\n                \"attribute\": {\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\n                },\n                \"operator\": {\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\n                },\n                \"valueFromUser\": {\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\n                },\n                \"notes\": {\n                  \"type\": \"string\"\n                }\n              },\n              \"required\": [\n                \"attribute\",\n                \"operator\",\n                \"valueFromUser\",\n                \"notes\"\n              ],\n              \"additionalProperties\": false\n            }\n          },\n          \"fieldsToReturn\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n              \"type\": \"string\"\n            }\n          },\n          \"sortBy\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ]\n          },\n          \"sortOrder\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"enum\": [\n                  \"asc\",\n                  \"desc\"\n                ]\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ]\n          },\n          \"dateFilterStrategy\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n              }\n            ]\n          },\n          \"llmSkillKey\": {\n            \"type\": \"null\"\n          },\n          \"llmPurpose\": {\n            \"type\": \"null\"\n          },\n          \"usePromptInstruction\": {\n            \"type\": \"null\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"required\": [\n          \"stepId\",\n          \"stepType\",\n          \"dependsOn\",\n          \"api\",\n          \"shapeKey\",\n          \"queryType\",\n          \"showTable\",\n          \"header\",\n          \"purpose\",\n          \"intentSliceKey\",\n          \"filters\",\n          \"fieldsToReturn\",\n          \"sortBy\",\n          \"sortOrder\",\n          \"dateFilterStrategy\",\n          \"llmSkillKey\",\n          \"llmPurpose\",\n          \"usePromptInstruction\"\n        ],\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"planMode\",\n    \"clarificationRequired\",\n    \"clarificationMessage\",\n    \"unsupportedQuery\",\n    \"unsupportedReason\",\n    \"plan\"\n  ],\n  \"additionalProperties\": false\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
